<worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="sec-block-matrices">
    <title>1.1 Block Matrices</title>

    <page>

        <p> A <term> block matrix </term> is a matrix that is interpreted as having been broken into sections called <term> blocks </term>, or <term> submatrices </term>.
        Intuitively, a block matrix can be interpreted as the original matrix that is partitioned into a collection of smaller matrices. For example, the matrix </p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 2
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> can also be written as a 2 &#215; 2 partitioned (or block) matrix: </p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{1,1} \amp A_{1,2} \\
                A_{2,1} \amp A_{2,2}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> where the entries of <term> A </term> are the blocks </p>

        <me>
            A_{1,1} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \quad
            A_{1,2} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \quad
            A_{2,1} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
            \quad
            A_{2,2} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp 2 \amp 2
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> We partitioned our matrix into four blocks, each of which have different dimensions. But the matrix could also, for example, be partitioned into five 4 &#215; 1 blocks,
        or four 1 &#215; 5 blocks. Indeed, matrices can be partitioned into blocks in many different ways, and depending on the application at hand, there can be a partitioning that
        is useful or needed. </p>

        <p> For example, when solving a linear system <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> to determine <m>\vec{x}</m>, we can construct and row reduce an augmented matrix of the
        form </p>

        <me>
            X =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A \amp \vec{b}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> The augmented matrix <term> X </term> consists of two sub-matrices, <term> A </term> and <m>\vec{b}</m>, meaning that it can be viewed as a block matrix.
        Another application of a block matrix arises when using the SVD, which is a popular tool used in data science. The SVD uses a matrix, <m>\Sigma</m>, of the form </p>

        <me>
            \Sigma =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                D \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp \mathbf{0}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> Matrix <term> D </term> is a diagonal matrix, and each <m>\mathbf{0}</m> is a zero matrix. Representing <m>\Sigma</m> in terms of sub-matrices helps us see what the structure
        of <m>\Sigma</m> is. Another block matrix arises when introducing a procedure for computing the inverse of an <m>n \times n</m> matrix. To compute the inverse of matrix <term> A
        </term>, we construct and row reduce the matrix </p>

        <me>
            X =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A \amp I
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> This is an example of a block matrix used in an algorithm. In order to use block matrices in other applications we need to define matrix addition and multiplication
        with partitioned matrices. </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.1 Block Matrix Addition}}</m>

        <p> If <m>m \times n</m> matrices <term> A </term> and <term> B </term> are partitioned in exactly the same way, then the entries of their sum is the sum of
        their blocks. For example, if <term> A </term> and <term> B </term> are the block matrices </p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{1,1} \amp A_{1,2} \\
                A_{2,1} \amp A_{2,2}
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            B =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                B_{1,1} \amp B_{1,2} \\
                B_{2,1} \amp B_{2,2}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> then their sum is the matrix </p>

        <me>
            A + B =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{1,1} + B_{1,1} \amp A_{1,2} + B_{1,2} \\
                A_{2,1} + B_{2,1} \amp A_{2,2} + B_{2,2}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> As long as <term> A </term> and <term> B </term> are partitioned in the same way the addition is calculated block by block. </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.2 Block Matrix Multiplication}}</m>

        <p> Recall the row column method for matrix multiplication. </p>

        <m>{\large \textbf{Theorem}}</m>

        <p>Let <m>A</m> be <m>m \times n</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>n \times p</m> matrix.
        Then, the <m>(i, j)</m> entry of <m>AB</m> is </p>

        <me>
            \operatorname{row}_i(A) \cdot \operatorname{col}_j(B).
        </me>

        <p> This is the Row Column Method for matrix multiplication. </p>

        <p> Partitioned matrices can be multiplied using this method, as if each block were a scalar provided each block has appropriate dimensions
        so that products are defined. </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.3 Example 1: Computing }A^2}</m>

        <p>Block matrices can be useful in cases where a matrix has a particular structure.</p>

        <p>For example, suppose <m>A</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> block matrix</p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                X \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp Y
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p> where <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are <m>p \times p</m> matrices, <m>\mathbf{0}</m> is a <m>p \times p</m> zero matrix, and <m>2p = n</m>. Then</p>

        <me>
            A^2 = AA =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                X \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp Y
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                X \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp Y
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                X^2 \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp Y^2
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>
            Computation of <m>A^2</m> only requires computing <m>X^2</m> and <m>Y^2</m>. Taking advantage of the block structure <m>A</m> leads to a more efficient computation than it otherwise would have been with a naive row-column method that does not take advantage of the structure of the matrix.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.4 Example 2: Computing }AB}</m>

        <p><m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are the matrices</p>

        <me>
            A =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11} \amp A_{12}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            B =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                B_{11} \\
                B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>where</p>

        <me>
            A_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            A_{12} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \\
                1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            B_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix},
            \quad
            B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>If we compute the matrix product using the given partitioning we obtain</p>

        <me>
            AB =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11} \amp A_{12}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                B_{11} \\
                B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A_{11}B_{11} + A_{12}B_{21}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>where</p>

        <me>
            A_{11}B_{11} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \qquad
            A_{12}B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \\
                1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>Therefore</p>

        <me>
            AB =
            A_{11}B_{11} + A_{12}B_{21} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1
            \end{pmatrix} +
            \begin{pmatrix}
                0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>Computing <m>AB</m> with the row column method confirms our result. </p>

        <me>
            AB =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 + 0 + 0 \amp -1 + 0 + 1 \\
                0 + 0 + 0 \amp 0 - 1 + 1
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                2 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.5 Block Matrix Inversion}}</m>

        <p>
            In some cases, matrix partitioning can be used to give us convenient expressions for the inverse of a matrix. Recall that the inverse of <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is a matrix <m>B</m>, that has the same dimensions as <m>A</m> and satisfies
        </p>

        <me>
            AB = BA = I
        </me>

        <p>where <m>I</m> is the <m>n \times n</m> identity matrix. As we will see in the next example, we can use this equation to construct expressions for the inverse of a matrix.</p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.6 Example 3: Expression for Inverse of a Block Matrix}}</m>

        <p>Recall, using our formula for a 2 &#215; 2 matrix, </p>

        <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
                a \amp b \\
                0 \amp c
            \end{pmatrix}^{-1} =
            \frac{1}{ac}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                c \amp -b \\
                0 \amp a
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                1/a \amp -b/(ac) \\
                0 \amp 1/c
            \end{pmatrix}
            \quad \quad \quad \text{(1.1)}
        </me>

        <p>
            provided that <m>ac \neq 0</m>. Suppose <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> are invertible <m>n \times n</m> matrices. Suppose we wish to construct an expression for the inverse of the matrix
        </p>

        <me>
            P =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A \amp B \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp C
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>To construct the inverse of <m>P</m>, we can write </p>

        <me>
            PP^{-1} = P^{-1}P = I_n
        </me>

        <p>where <m>P^{-1}</m> is the matrix we seek. If we let <m>P^{-1}</m> be the block matrix </p>

        <me>
            P^{-1} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                W \amp X \\
                Y \amp Z
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>we can determine <m>P^{-1}</m> by solving <m>PP^{-1} = I_n</m> or <m>P^{-1}P = I_n</m>. Solving <m>PP^{-1} = I_n</m> gives us: </p>

        <me>
            I_n = PP^{-1} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                I \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp I
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A \amp B \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp C
            \end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                W \amp X \\
                Y \amp Z
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
                I \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp I
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                AW + BY \amp AX + BZ \\
                CY \amp CZ
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>
            The above matrix equation gives us a set of four equations that can be solved to determine <m>W</m>, <m>X</m>, <m>Y</m>, and <m>Z</m>. The block in the second row and first column gives us <m>CY = \mathbf{0}</m>. It was given that <m>C</m> is an invertible matrix, so <m>Y</m> is a zero matrix because
        </p>

        <me>CY = \mathbf{0}</me>
        <me>C^{-1}CY = C^{-1}\mathbf{0}</me>
        <me>IY = \mathbf{0}</me>
        <me>Y = \mathbf{0}</me>

        <p>Likewise the block in the second row and second column yields <m>CZ = I</m>, so </p>

        <me>CZ = I</me>
        <me>C^{-1}CZ = C^{-1}I</me>
        <me>Z = C^{-1}</me>

        <p>Now that we have expressions for <m>Y</m> and <m>Z</m> we can solve the remaining two equations for <m>W</m> and <m>X</m>. Solving for <m>X</m> gives us the following expression.</p>

        <me>AX + BZ = \mathbf{0}</me>
        <me>AX + BC^{-1} = \mathbf{0}</me>
        <me>AX = -BC^{-1}</me>
        <me>A^{-1}AX = -A^{-1}BC^{-1}</me>
        <me>X = -A^{-1}BC^{-1}</me>

        <p>Solving for <m>W</m>: </p>

        <me>AW + BY = I</me>
        <me>AW + B\mathbf{0} = I</me>
        <me>A^{-1}AW = A^{-1}I</me>
        <me>W = A^{-1}</me>

        <p>We now have our expression for <m>P^{-1}</m>: </p>

        <me>
            P^{-1} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                W \amp X \\
                Y \amp Z
            \end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}
                A^{-1} \amp -A^{-1}BC^{-1} \\
                \mathbf{0} \amp C^{-1}
            \end{pmatrix}
        </me>

        <p>Note that in the special case where <m>n = 2</m> that each of the blocks are scalars and our expression is equivalent to Equation (1.1).</p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.7 Summary}}</m>

        <p>
            In this section we used partitioned matrices to solve problems regarding matrix invertibility and matrix multiplication. Partitioned matrices can be multiplied using this method, as if each block were a scalar provided each block has appropriate dimensions so that products are defined. They can be used for example when dealing with large matrices that have a known structure where it is more convenient to describe the structure of a matrix in terms of its blocks. Although not part of this text, matrix partitioning can be used to help derive new algorithms because they give a more concise representation of a matrix and of operations on matrices.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <m>{\large \textbf{1.1.8 Exercises}}</m>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A = \begin{pmatrix} Y \amp X \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} X \amp \mathbf{0} \\ Y \amp Z \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} X \\ Y \end{pmatrix}</m>. Which of the following could <m>A</m> be equal to?
            </p>

            <p>(a) <m>A = YX^{2} + XYX + XZY</m></p>
            <p>(b) <m>A = 2X + XZY</m></p>
            <p>(c) <m>A = YX^{2} + X + Z</m></p>
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <p>
                <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>n \times n</m> invertible matrices. Construct expressions for <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> in terms of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
            </p>

            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix}
                    \mathbf{0} \amp X \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                    A \amp \mathbf{0} \amp Y
                \end{pmatrix}
                \begin{pmatrix}
                    B \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                    \mathbf{0} \amp A \\
                    A \amp \mathbf{0}
                \end{pmatrix} =
                \begin{pmatrix}
                    \mathbf{0} \amp B \\
                    A \amp \mathbf{0}
                \end{pmatrix}
            </me>
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1in">
            <p>
                Suppose <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are invertible <m>n \times n</m> matrices, and
            </p>

            <me>
                P =
                \begin{pmatrix}
                    A \amp \mathbf{0} \\
                    B \amp C
                \end{pmatrix}
            </me>

            <p>
                Give an expression for <m>P^{-1}</m> in terms of <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
            </p>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>
